<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>MUI.Crawler</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>MUI.Crawler</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The join. MUI.Crawl produces a ProbeResult, MUI.Catalog stores what one means, MUI.Discovery
    decides who to dial and who a probe is, and until this project existed nothing connected them.

    This is deliberately the ONLY assembly that references all three, and the arrows below it stay
    exactly as they were: MUI.Catalog still cannot see MUI.Crawl, so every writer downstream of a
    probe is still testable against a captured fixture with no socket anywhere (spec §6.5).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MUI.Catalog\MUI.Catalog.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MUI.Crawl\MUI.Crawl.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MUI.Discovery\MUI.Discovery.csproj" />

    <!--
      The second pipe. MUI.I3 reads a sidecar the same way MUI.Crawl reads a socket, and neither can
      see MUI.Catalog, so the arrows are unchanged: this is still the only assembly that knows both
      what came back and what it means.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MUI.I3\MUI.I3.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql" />
    <PackageReference Include="Dapper" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />

    <!--
      The crawler is an in-process BackgroundService in the web deployable (spec §4.11, §12), so it
      takes the hosting and DI *abstractions* and nothing else. A library that pulled in the hosting
      implementation would be deciding how its host is built.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />

    <!--
      §11's opt-out reads a TXT record, which the BCL cannot ask for. The interface lives in
      MUI.Discovery beside the gate that consults it and the implementation lives here, exactly as
      IHostResolver and its live counterpart do — so the projects that hold the rules stay free of a
      resolver, and no test anywhere depends on somebody else's zone file.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="DnsClient" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
